Typically when ISA's transition 32bit - > 64bit these are the areas that are impacted:

-> 64bit pointers
-> 64bit registers
-> enhanced instructions

-> calling convention 
-> ABI compatibility

Choices:
-> After transition theres generally 3 modes:
   --> OLD_ISA32
   --> NEW_ISA32
   --> NEW_ISA64

-> Most processors will let NEW boxes run OLD_ISA32. NEW_ISA32 never works on old boxes.

-> The real question is whether there is an explicit NEW_ISA32 (ARM does its AARCH32 
   SPARC does its called V8PLUS x86 doesn't until x32abi kicks off. Its not clear to me
   how MIPS handles this lets ask Jeff)

-> Ideally you want to recompile 32bit code to NEW_ISA32

-> NEW_ISA32 typically disallows 64bit pointers but it depends on whether 64bit registers 
   are made available. New instructions are always available. SPARCv8PLUS allowed 64bit
   registers, folks are debating creating x32 [1]


